Section 5: Asynchronous Processing & Background Jobs
Transactional Outbox Pattern: To guarantee that secondary tasks (like mocked
notifications or data enrichment) are processed reliably without risking data
inconsistency if the main transaction fails, the agent must implement a
Transactional Outbox pattern.
PDF + 1
Atomic Job Creation: When a primary action occurs (e.g., creating a new item
or reassigning an owner), the server must insert a job record into an outbox or
jobs table within the exact same database transaction as the primary update.
PDF + 1
Reliable Workers: A background worker (e.g., using Redis with a tool like
BullMQ, Celery, or rq) must independently read these outbox records to execute
the secondary work.
PDF + 1
Failure Handling & Retries: The architecture must account for worker failures.
The worker must implement automatic retries with exponential backoff and a
strict maximum attempt limit. If a job repeatedly fails, it must be marked as failed
rather than crashing the worker loop.
PDF + 3
Idempotent Handlers: Because network delays or worker restarts can cause a
job to be processed more than once, the asynchronous handler must be
designed to be idempotent. Processing a job twice must result in only a single
mocked notification or side effect.
